## What a `type: anti-pattern` instance is

A named, reusable **negative convention** — the wrong move in a class of situations, why it's attractive despite being wrong, and what to do instead.  Every anti-pattern must cite at least one positive pattern as the alternative.

Anti-patterns are the **don't-do-these**.  Their value is in making the temptation explicit so future collaborators recognize it before falling in.  *"Don't grep when xref knows"* is more useful than silence because it names the specific temptation (grep).

Anti-pattern is a subtype of [`guidance`](guidance.md) — memorials that prescribe action (negative prescription here).

## When to create an anti-pattern memorial

- You catch yourself — or another collaborator — doing the wrong thing, and the temptation has a recurring shape worth naming
- A principal explicitly flags a failure mode (*"don't do X"* / *"stop doing Y"*)
- A pattern memorial has grown a "When NOT to apply" section large enough to deserve its own inverted memorial
- A failure mode keeps showing up across sessions and the corrective signal isn't sticking

## When NOT to create one

- The wrong move is obvious and nobody would actually do it; anti-patterns earn their keep on things that are **tempting**
- The rule is a project-specific one-off (use `feedback` instead)
- The anti-pattern's positive alternative doesn't have a named pattern yet — write the pattern first, then the anti-pattern can cite it

